pub fn run<F>(_app_id: &str, title: &str, ring: Rc<RefCell<RingTransport>>, on_event: F)
where
    F: FnMut(Rc<RefCell<RingTransport>>) + 'static,
{
    struct RingPump(Rc<RefCell<RingTransport>>);
    impl Pump for RingPump {
        fn next_frame(&mut self) -> Result<Option<OpcodeBatch<'_>>, TransportError> {
            FrameSource::next_frame(&mut *self.0.borrow_mut())
        }
        fn send_input(&mut self, event: &Event) -> Result<(), TransportError> {
            DriverTransport::send_input(&mut *self.0.borrow_mut(), event)
        }
    }

    let ring_for_cb = ring.clone();
    HOST_ACTION.with(|h| {
        let mut f = on_event;
        *h.borrow_mut() = Some(Box::new(move || f(ring_for_cb.clone())));
    });

    run_with_pump_sized(
        title,
        Box::new(RingPump(ring)),
        Some(host_action_cb),
        420,
        220,
    );
}
```

The borrow in `next_frame`: `&mut *self.0.borrow_mut()` — the returned `OpcodeBatch` borrows the ring memory. The RefMut guard `self.0.borrow_mut()` is a temporary; `&mut *guard` creates a &mut RingTransport; `FrameSource::next_frame(&mut *guard)` returns `Result<Option<OpcodeBatch<'_>>, _>` where the batch's lifetime is tied to the `&mut RingTransport` — which is tied to the temporary guard. That would be a lifetime error! The temporary guard is dropped at the end of the statement, but the batch outlives it.

