Phase E CI done (will need a CI run to verify — I can't run CI locally, but I can simulate the Linux build check? No, I'm on macOS. I'll note that CI verification needs a push. But I can at least verify the build.rs Linux path logic is sound. The apt package names: `qml6-module-qtquick`, `qml6-module-qtquick-controls`, `qml6-module-qtquick-templates`, `qml6-module-qtquick-window2` — these are valid Ubuntu packages for Qt6 (for qtquick controls they exist: `qml6-module-qtquick-controls`). Good.

Now Phase A. Let me start with the event plumbing (A1, A2, A3 all touch the C++ bridge + Rust event path).

Let me plan the concrete changes:

### pathland_qt.h
Add event kinds:
```c
PLQT_EV_POINTER_DOWN = 4,
PLQT_EV_POINTER_MOVE = 5,
PLQT_EV_NAVIGATE_BACK = 6,
PLQT_EV_SCHEME_CHANGED = 7,
```
(POINTER_UP already = 1, VALUE_CHANGED=2, TEXT_CHANGED=3)

### bridge.h
Add slots:
```cpp
void pointer(quint32 nodeId, quint32 kind, qreal x, qreal y);
void navigateBack();
void schemeChanged(quint32 scheme);
```
And a `std::unordered_map<uint32_t,uint32_t>* listeners` pointer? The pointer events gate by EVENT_LISTENERS mask which the MouseArea handlers already filter (only attached handlers fire). So bridge.pointer doesn't need the mask. But `fire()` gates by `gated` set — pointer events must bypass that. Add a `firePointer` that only checks suppress.

Wait — should pointer events check suppress? Pointer events are user-generated, never suppressed. So firePointer checks nothing (or just event_fn presence).

For navigateBack: global, no gating, node_id=0. fireGlobal → event_fn directly.

For schemeChanged: global, no gating, node_id=0, value=scheme.

### bridge.cpp
```cpp
void Bridge::pointer(quint32 nodeId, quint32 kind, qreal x, qreal y) {
    PathlandQtEvent ev{};
    ev.kind = (uint8_t)kind;
    ev.node_id = nodeId;
    ev.x = x; ev.y = y;
    if (event_fn) event_fn(&ev, user);
}
void Bridge::navigateBack() {
    PathlandQtEvent ev{}; ev.kind = PLQT_EV_NAVIGATE_BACK; ev.node_id = 0;
    if (event_fn) event_fn(&ev, user);
}
void Bridge::schemeChanged(quint32 scheme) {
    PathlandQtEvent ev{}; ev.kind = PLQT_EV_SCHEME_CHANGED; ev.node_id = 0; ev.value = (float)scheme;
    if (event_fn) event_fn(&ev, user);
}
```

### qt_layer.cpp
1. MouseArea attachment:
   - Track `listener_masks: unordered_map<uint32_t,uint32_t>` and `mouse_areas: unordered_map<uint32_t,QQuickItem*>`.
   - In applyProperty, handle PL_PROP_EVENT_LISTENERS (0x2005): 
     ```cpp
     case PL_PROP_EVENT_LISTENERS: {
         g.listener_masks[id] = value;
         attachPointerArea(id, value);
         break;
     }
     ```
   - `attachPointerArea(id, mask)`:
     ```cpp
     auto it = g.mouse_areas.find(id);
     if (it != g.mouse_areas.end()) { it->second->deleteLater(); g.mouse_areas.erase(it); }
     uint32_t bits = mask & (1|2|4);  // DOWN|MOVE|UP
     if (!bits) return;
     QQuickItem* item = findWidget(id);
     if (!item) return;
     QString handlers;
     if (mask & 1) handlers += "onPressed: bridge.pointer(" + id + ", 4, mouse.x, mouse.y)\n";
     if (mask & 2) handlers += "onPositionChanged: bridge.pointer(" + id + ", 5, mouse.x, mouse.y)\n";
     if (mask & 4) handlers += "onReleased: bridge.pointer(" + id + ", 1, mouse.x, mouse.y)\n";
     QString qml = "import QtQuick 2.15\nMouseArea { anchors.fill: parent; " + handlers + " }";
     QQuickItem* ma = createComponent(g.engine, item, qml.toUtf8());
     if (ma) g.mouse_areas[id] = ma;
     ```
     Note: kind codes passed to bridge.pointer must be the PLQT_EV_* values (4=down, 5=move, 1=up). Let me use those.
   - Also handle: on RESET_NODE, remove the MouseArea + listener mask? RESET currently resets style. Listeners — the diff re-sends full props including EVENT_LISTENERS after RESET, so applyProperty will re-attach. If RESET doesn't remove the old MouseArea, we'd get duplicates. Let me have resetNode remove the MouseArea + mask. Actually simpler: resetNode calls a helper `detachPointerArea(id)`.

2. Escape → NAVIGATE:
   - Add a `KeyFilter : public QObject` event filter on the window in init.
   - `fireNavigateBack` → `g.bridge->navigateBack()`.
   - Escape only (BackSpace deferred, documented).
   - `QKeyEvent` include `<QtGui/QKeyEvent>`.

3. Scheme detection:
   - In init, after app creation: `QObject::connect(g.app->styleHints(), &QStyleHints::colorSchemeChanged, g.app, [event_fn, user]() { fireScheme(event_fn, user); })`. But the bridge isn't wired yet at init — it is (bridge created before). Let me use `g.bridge->schemeChanged(...)`. Actually the callback needs to fire with the current scheme. The Bridge has event_fn+user. Let me add a helper in qt_layer.cpp:
     ```cpp
     void reportScheme() {
         QStyleHints* hints = g.app->styleHints();
         quint32 s = (hints->colorScheme() == Qt::ColorScheme::Dark) ? 1 : 0;
         if (g.bridge) g.bridge->schemeChanged(s);
     }
     ```
   - Connect: `QObject::connect(hints, &QStyleHints::colorSchemeChanged, g.app, []() { reportScheme(); });`
   - Call reportScheme() once at end of init (after window setup).
   - Include `<QtGui/QStyleHints>`, `<QtGui/QGuiApplication>`.

### ffi.rs (Rust)
Add event kinds:
```rust
pub mod ev {
    pub const POINTER_UP: u8 = 1;
    pub const VALUE_CHANGED: u8 = 2;
    pub const TEXT_CHANGED: u8 = 3;
    pub const POINTER_DOWN: u8 = 4;
    pub const POINTER_MOVE: u8 = 5;
    pub const NAVIGATE_BACK: u8 = 6;
    pub const SCHEME_CHANGED: u8 = 7;
}
```

### run.rs (Rust)
Update `qt_event` (both Runner and RingRunner) to handle:
- POINTER_DOWN → Event::PointerDown { target, x, y, secondary:false }
- POINTER_MOVE → Event::PointerMove { target, x, y, hovering:false, leaving:false }
- POINTER_UP → Event::PointerUp
- NAVIGATE_BACK → Event::Navigate { url: None }
- SCHEME_CHANGED → renderer.set_scheme(Light/Dark) and DON'T wake host / don't send_input.

Wait — SCHEME_CHANGED needs the renderer, not the pump. Both Runner and RingRunner have `renderer`. So qt_event needs to distinguish. Let me restructure: the event match returns an enum `Dispatch`:
```rust
enum QtAction { Send(Event), Scheme(Scheme), None }
```
Then:
- Send → pump.send_input + wake
- Scheme(s) → runner.renderer.set_scheme(s) (no wake)
- None → nothing

For scheme: `pathland_core::tokens::Scheme`. The renderer needs `QtRenderer::set_scheme`. Let me add it:
```rust
pub fn set_scheme(&mut self, scheme: Scheme) {
    self.tree.set_scheme(scheme);
    let cmds = self.diff();
    crate::ffi::apply(&cmds);
}
```
Wait — RenderTree.set_scheme re-resolves tokens, mutating node.properties/strings. Then diff emits SET_PROPERTY for changed nodes. Good.

The diff needs `&mut self`. set_scheme takes &mut self. Fine.

But — there's a subtlety: after set_scheme, node.properties for token-referencing nodes change → diff emits RESET+full props for those nodes. Good.

### renderer.rs
Add `set_scheme`.

### A4: META::RESET in pump
In pump_once (Runner):
```rust
fn pump_once(runner: &mut Runner) {
    match runner.pump.next_frame() {
        Ok(Some(batch)) => {
            let frame = batch.frame();
            if frame_has_reset(frame) { runner.renderer.reset(); } else { runner.renderer.apply_frame(frame); }
        }
        ...
    }
}
fn frame_has_reset(frame: &Frame) -> bool {
    frame.opcodes().any(|op| op.category() == category::META && op.command() == meta::RESET)
}
```
Need `pathland_core::{category, meta}` imports. Same for pump_once_ring.

Wait — `batch.frame()` borrows the batch; apply_frame borrows tree. Fine.

### A5: demo slider binding
Let me check the Rust DSL for property binding. Search for `value_binding` or how Slider can bind to a signal. The Node has `property_bindings: BTreeMap<u16, ...>`. Let me look at how the DSL binds a property to a signal.

Let me check the pathland_view for a binding API for properties.

Let me now write all the code. Start with the C header, bridge, qt_layer, then Rust.

Actually — one concern with the scheme callback: at init, the QML engine + bridge exist, but reportScheme fires before the window shows. The renderer's set_scheme emits SET_PROPERTY commands to a scene that doesn't have widgets yet (no apply yet). The diff will emit commands referencing node ids that C++ doesn't have → applyProperty finds no widget → ignores. Then the first frame apply creates widgets with the resolved (scheme-appropriate) property values anyway (diff compares sent, which... the diff after set_scheme updates sent to the new values; then apply_frame for the first frame: tree already has scheme-applied values; diff vs sent → no property commands. But the CREATE commands carry the node; C++ creates widgets and applies... wait, the initial diff emits create + reset + full props (because sent empty). The full props are the scheme-resolved values. So it works out: set_scheme before the first frame marks sent with the resolved values; the first frame's create+props then carries them. Actually no: set_scheme on an empty renderer (tree empty) does nothing (no nodes). Then the first frame populates the tree; diff emits create + full props (resolved against the scheme set on RenderTree). So scheme is baked into the first emission. Good — no ordering problem. And scheme changes later re-resolve + re-apply. 

Let me also double check `RenderTree::set_scheme` is public in render-core — yes, `pub fn set_scheme(&mut self, scheme: Scheme)`. Good.

Now — the `QtEvent` struct is `#[repr(C)]`; I add kinds as consts only, no struct change needed (value field already exists).

Let me also make sure the C header's PathlandQtEvent has a `value` field for scheme (yes it does).

Let me write the code now.

One more: for the MouseArea, `anchors.fill: parent` — the MouseArea's parent is the item. `anchors.fill: parent` inside the QML string refers to the MouseArea's parent item. Good.

But there's a subtlety: for a Text item, the MouseArea as child with anchors.fill — Text has no visual rect by default (implicit size). The MouseArea fills the Text's size. If Text is HUG (implicit), it has content size. Fine.

Also `z`: MouseArea should be on top. Default is fine.

Let me also consider: pointer events for buttons — the GTK demo's "Text with raw pointer listeners" uses `.pointer_events(listener::POINTER_DOWN | POINTER_UP)`. So down and up. Good.

Now let me implement. Start with pathland_qt.h.

